  "question": "If $f(x)=\\sin^{-1}x$, find $f'\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$.",
  "answeroption": "B",
  "explanation": "<b>Derivative of $\\sin^{-1}x$</b><br>For $-1<x<1$, $$f'(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$$<br><b>Substitution of $x=\\frac{1}{2}$</b><br>$$f'\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}}}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$$<br>Hence, $$f'\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.$$",
